### What is the problem?

Build a FIFO queue using only two LIFO stacks. Enqueue adds to the back; dequeue removes from the front. The challenge is doing this efficiently, not naively reversing on every operation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class TwoStackQueue<T> {
    private final Deque<T> inbox = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<T> outbox = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void enqueue(T item) { inbox.push(item); }

    public T dequeue() {
        if (outbox.isEmpty()) {
            while (!inbox.isEmpty()) outbox.push(inbox.pop());
        }
        return outbox.pop();
    }

    public T peek() {
        if (outbox.isEmpty()) {
            while (!inbox.isEmpty()) outbox.push(inbox.pop());
        }
        return outbox.peek();
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() { return inbox.isEmpty() && outbox.isEmpty(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TwoStackQueue<String> q = new TwoStackQueue<>();
        q.enqueue("TXN-A"); q.enqueue("TXN-B"); q.enqueue("TXN-C");
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // TXN-A
        q.enqueue("TXN-D");
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // TXN-B
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // TXN-C
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // TXN-D
    }
}
```

```text
TXN-A
TXN-B
TXN-C
TXN-D
```

### Why does transferring reverse the order?

Popping from one stack and pushing to another reverses the element order. The bottom of the first stack ends up on top of the second. This turns LIFO into FIFO for that batch.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class ReverseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<String> in = new ArrayDeque<>();
        in.push("First"); in.push("Second"); in.push("Third");

        Deque<String> out = new ArrayDeque<>();
        while (!in.isEmpty()) out.push(in.pop());

        System.out.println(out.pop()); // First (oldest)
        System.out.println(out.pop()); // Second
        System.out.println(out.pop()); // Third
    }
}
```

```text
First
Second
Third
```

### What is the amortized analysis?

Each element goes through three moves: push to inbox (O(1)), transfer to outbox (O(1)), pop from outbox (O(1)). Total per element is O(3) = O(1). A single dequeue that triggers a transfer can be O(n), but spread across all operations the average is O(1).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class AmortizedCost {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Per element:");
        System.out.println("  1. Push to inbox: O(1)");
        System.out.println("  2. Move to outbox: O(1) (once in lifetime)");
        System.out.println("  3. Pop from outbox: O(1)");
        System.out.println("Total per element: O(1) amortized");
        System.out.println("Single worst-case dequeue: O(n) but rare");
    }
}
```

```text
Per element:
  1. Push to inbox: O(1)
  2. Move to outbox: O(1) (once in lifetime)
  3. Pop from outbox: O(1)
Total per element: O(1) amortized
Single worst-case dequeue: O(n) but rare
```

### Why is lazy transfer critical?

Transferring only when the outbox is empty ensures each element moves exactly once. If you transfer eagerly on every dequeue, newly enqueued elements get mixed with older ones in the outbox, breaking both FIFO order and the amortized guarantee.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class LazyVsEager {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Lazy: transfer only when outbox empty");
        System.out.println("  Element moves inbox->outbox exactly once");
        System.out.println("  FIFO preserved, amortized O(1)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Eager: transfer on every dequeue");
        System.out.println("  Newly enqueued items mixed into outbox");
        System.out.println("  FIFO broken, no amortized guarantee");
    }
}
```

```text
Lazy: transfer only when outbox empty
  Element moves inbox->outbox exactly once
  FIFO preserved, amortized O(1)

Eager: transfer on every dequeue
  Newly enqueued items mixed into outbox
  FIFO broken, no amortized guarantee
```

### What are common mistakes?

The biggest mistake is eager transfer. Another is forgetting to check both stacks in `isEmpty()`. A third is using this in production Java instead of simply using `ArrayDeque` as a queue.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TwoStackMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Mistake 1: eager transfer -> breaks order and complexity");
        System.out.println("Mistake 2: isEmpty checks only inbox or only outbox");
        System.out.println("Mistake 3: using this in production instead of ArrayDeque");
    }
}
```

```text
Mistake 1: eager transfer -> breaks order and complexity
Mistake 2: isEmpty checks only inbox or only outbox
Mistake 3: using this in production instead of ArrayDeque
```

### What are the edge cases?

Dequeue on an empty queue should throw or return null. Multiple enqueues followed by multiple dequeues should return in FIFO order. Interleaved enqueues and dequeues should still maintain order because the outbox holds older elements.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TwoStackEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TwoStackQueue<String> q = new TwoStackQueue<>();
        // Interleaved operations
        q.enqueue("A"); q.enqueue("B");
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // A
        q.enqueue("C");
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // B (from outbox, before C)
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // C
        System.out.println("Empty: " + q.isEmpty()); // true
    }
}
```

```text
A
B
C
Empty: true
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At the National Bank of India, a legacy component exposed only stack primitives, but the system needed strict oldest-first fairness for customer service requests. The two-stack queue gave FIFO semantics over stack primitives, and the lazy transfer kept throughput high during burst processing.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ServiceRequestQueue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TwoStackQueue<String> requests = new TwoStackQueue<>();
        requests.enqueue("REQ-001: Rajesh account inquiry");
        requests.enqueue("REQ-002: Priya loan status");
        requests.enqueue("REQ-003: Pankaj balance check");

        System.out.println("Serving: " + requests.dequeue());
        System.out.println("Serving: " + requests.dequeue());
        requests.enqueue("REQ-004: Rajesh card block");
        System.out.println("Serving: " + requests.dequeue());
        System.out.println("Serving: " + requests.dequeue());
    }
}
```

```text
Serving: REQ-001: Rajesh account inquiry
Serving: REQ-002: Priya loan status
Serving: REQ-003: Pankaj balance check
Serving: REQ-004: Rajesh card block
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

In production Java use `ArrayDeque` directly. The two-stack queue matters when the only primitive is a stack, such as implementing a queue over an immutable stack in functional code. It underpins the Banker's queue in functional data structures where two lists replace the two stacks. The amortized analysis is what interviewers really test.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorTwoStackNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Production: use ArrayDeque as a queue");
        System.out.println("This pattern: when only stacks available");
        System.out.println("Functional: Banker's queue with two lists");
        System.out.println("Interview key: explain amortized O(1)");
    }
}
```

```text
Production: use ArrayDeque as a queue
This pattern: when only stacks available
Functional: Banker's queue with two lists
Interview key: explain amortized O(1)
```
